---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Hop — the network that finds a way"
  description="Hop is a delay-tolerant mesh network for intermittently-connected devices. Sealed messages relay device to device over Bluetooth until they reach the internet — or the person you’re trying to reach. Free SDK, paid cloud backbone."
  path="/"
>
  <!-- hero -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="eyebrow">Delay-tolerant mesh networking</div>
      <h1>The network that <span class="grad">finds a way.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Hop relays sealed messages from device to device over Bluetooth until they
        reach the internet — or the person you're trying to reach. No tower, no
        Wi-Fi, no infrastructure required. One shared mesh, embedded in every app.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/developers/">Build with Hop</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/business/">For business</a>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-meta">
        <span class="tag"><b>Offline-first</b> — works with zero connectivity</span>
        <span class="tag"><b>Sealed end-to-end</b> — relays can't read it</span>
        <span class="tag"><b>Rust core</b> — iOS &amp; Android</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- the two problems -->
  <section class="band band-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div class="eyebrow">Why Hop exists</div>
        <h2>Connectivity fails. Messages shouldn't.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Most networks assume an unbroken path from sender to receiver, right now.
          Hop assumes the opposite — and still gets through, eventually, every time.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="ic">📵</div>
          <h3>You're offline. The answer isn't.</h3>
          <p>A device with no internet emits a sealed request. It hops across nearby
             devices until one with a connection fulfills it and relays the response
             back the same way.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="ic">🧑‍🤝‍🧑</div>
          <h3>No infrastructure — just people.</h3>
          <p>Two people, no cell network at all. A message hops device to device
             across everyone in between until it reaches its destination — sealed the
             whole way.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="ic">🌍</div>
          <h3>Distance is no object.</h3>
          <p>The moment any carrier touches the internet, Hop parks the sealed message
             in a shared store and a device near the far island picks it up. One
             internet leg becomes one very long hop.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- how it flows -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div class="eyebrow">How it works</div>
        <h2>Store-and-forward, hop by hop.</h2>
        <p>Every message is a self-contained, signed, sealed <strong>bundle</strong>.
           Relays carry ciphertext they cannot read; only the destination can open it.
           Binary spray-and-wait spreads a small copy budget across independent paths —
           the first to arrive delivers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flow">
        <div class="hop"><span class="glyph">📱</span><small>You · offline</small></div>
        <div class="arr"></div>
        <div class="hop"><span class="glyph">📱</span><small>Relay</small></div>
        <div class="arr"></div>
        <div class="hop"><span class="glyph">📱</span><small>Relay</small></div>
        <div class="arr"></div>
        <div class="hop gw"><span class="glyph">🛰️</span><small>Gateway</small></div>
        <div class="arr"></div>
        <div class="hop dst"><span class="glyph">🌐</span><small>Internet / peer</small></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- the shared fabric -->
  <section class="band band-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="grid-2" style="align-items:center; gap:40px;">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">One shared fabric</div>
          <h2>Embed once. Relay for everyone.</h2>
          <p class="mt-s">Hop is a Rust library you embed — not an app you adopt. Every
             Hop-enabled app relays for every other, so no app is ever alone on the
             network. The more apps that ship it, the denser the mesh becomes for all
             of them.</p>
          <p class="mt-s">And when any carrier touches the internet, the optional cloud
             backbone turns the whole world into one extra hop — region-aware, sealed,
             and scale-to-zero so it costs nothing when idle.</p>
          <div class="hero-cta">
            <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/developers/">See the protocol</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid-2" style="gap:14px;">
          <div class="card lit"><div class="ic">🔒</div><h3>Sealed end-to-end</h3><p>X25519 + ChaCha20-Poly1305. Relays forward, never read.</p></div>
          <div class="card"><div class="ic">🛰️</div><h3>Internet when available</h3><p>Online nodes park messages for far-away nodes to fetch.</p></div>
          <div class="card"><div class="ic">🧭</div><h3>Discover by gossip</h3><p>Find people and services the moment you meet anyone who's seen them.</p></div>
          <div class="card"><div class="ic">🔑</div><h3>The address is the key</h3><p>One Ed25519 key is your identity, your address, and your seal.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- the narrow waist / build on Hop -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div class="eyebrow">Build on Hop</div>
        <h2>One layer. Every offline app.</h2>
        <p>Most offline products rebuild the same transport — discovery, mesh,
           store-and-forward — and weld it to a single app. Hop is that layer, done once:
           a sealed, store-and-forward datagram every app can build on. The narrow waist
           for intermittent networks — many bearers below, many apps above.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="layerstack">
        <div class="lrow">
          <span class="lvl">Apps</span>
          <div class="chips">
            <span class="lchip">Messaging</span>
            <span class="lchip">Data sync</span>
            <span class="lchip">File transfer</span>
            <span class="lchip">Offline web</span>
            <span class="lchip">Telemetry</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lrow">
          <span class="lvl">Semantics</span>
          <div class="chips">
            <span class="lchip code">hops://</span>
            <span class="lchip code">hps://</span>
            <span class="lchip code">streams</span>
            <span class="lchip code">HNS</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lrow waist">
          <span class="lvl">Waist</span>
          <div class="chips">
            <span class="lchip code">hdp://</span>
            <span class="lchip">sealed, store-and-forward datagrams</span>
          </div>
          <span class="waisttag">the waist</span>
        </div>
        <div class="lrow bearer">
          <span class="lvl">Bearer</span>
          <div class="chips">
            <span class="lchip">Bluetooth LE</span>
            <span class="lchip">L2CAP</span>
            <span class="lchip">TCP / WebSocket</span>
            <span class="lchip">LoRa, Wi-Fi Aware…</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="note mt-m">A vertically-integrated offline app is the whole column. Hop is
         the waist — so a sync engine, a messenger, or a telemetry pipeline builds on it
         instead of rebuilding radios and store-and-forward.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- how it's monetized -->
  <section class="band band-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div class="eyebrow">The model</div>
        <h2>The library is free. The cloud is the product.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The device does the work, so the SDK is open and free. The cloud
           backbone costs money to run — so that's what you pay for, only when you use it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="layers">
        <div class="layer">
          <div class="lv">Layer 0</div>
          <h3>Open SDK</h3>
          <p>Embed <code>hop-core</code> on iOS &amp; Android. Source-available, free forever.</p>
          <div class="who">Free</div>
        </div>
        <div class="layer paid">
          <div class="lv">Layer 1</div>
          <h3>Hosted backbone</h3>
          <p>Managed relay fleet, durable mailboxes, internet egress, <code>hops://</code> endpoints.</p>
          <div class="who">Usage-based</div>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <div class="lv">Layer 2</div>
          <h3>Commercial license</h3>
          <p>Terms beyond the FSL non-compete, for products that need them.</p>
          <div class="who">Enterprise</div>
        </div>
        <div class="layer">
          <div class="lv">Layer 3</div>
          <h3>Private &amp; mission</h3>
          <p>On-prem fleets, private backbones, and support for field operations.</p>
          <div class="who">Solutions</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="note mt-m">Source-available under <a class="mono" style="color:var(--accent)" href="https://github.com/jwaldrip/hop/blob/main/LICENSE.md">FSL-1.1-ALv2</a> — converts to Apache 2.0 after two years.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- audience doors -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="doors">
        <a class="door" href="/developers/">
          <div class="kicker">For developers</div>
          <h3>Put your app on the mesh.</h3>
          <p>A pure-Rust core with iOS &amp; Android bindings, an HTTP-over-mesh layer
             (<code>hops://</code>), pub/sub, and DNSSEC-backed naming. Embed it and
             your app keeps working when the network doesn't.</p>
          <div class="go">Explore the SDK →</div>
        </a>
        <a class="door" href="/business/">
          <div class="kicker">For business</div>
          <h3>Connectivity that doesn't depend on connectivity.</h3>
          <p>Events, field operations, maritime, rural, response. Run on the managed
             backbone with regional reliability, or stand up a private fleet of your
             own. Licensing and support included.</p>
          <div class="go">See solutions →</div>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- cta -->
  <section class="cta-band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Start where the network ends.</h2>
      <p>Embed the free SDK today. Add the cloud backbone when you need to reach across the world.</p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/developers/">Build with Hop</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/pricing/">See pricing</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
